## 6. PRE-MIGRATION: security capture

### 6.1 All logins with SIDs and password hashes

```sql
SELECT
    sp.name,
    sp.principal_id,
    sp.type_desc,
    sp.is_disabled,
    sp.create_date,
    sp.modify_date,
    sp.default_database_name,
    sp.default_language_name,
    CONVERT(VARCHAR(MAX), sp.sid, 1) AS sid_hex,
    CONVERT(VARCHAR(MAX), sl.password_hash, 1) AS password_hash_hex,
    sl.is_policy_checked,
    sl.is_expiration_checked
FROM sys.server_principals sp
LEFT JOIN sys.sql_logins sl ON sp.principal_id = sl.principal_id
WHERE sp.type IN ('S','U','G')
  AND sp.name NOT LIKE '##%'
  AND sp.name NOT IN ('NT SERVICE\SQLWriter','NT SERVICE\Winmgmt')
ORDER BY sp.name;
```

To actually *script* the `CREATE LOGIN` statements with hashes preserved, use one of:

- `sp_help_revlogin` (Microsoft KB 918992) — generates scripts with `HASHED` keyword.
- dbatools `Export-DbaLogin -SqlInstance SOURCE -Path C:\Migration\logins.sql`.
- SMO PowerShell (below).

```powershell
# SMO-based login scripting preserving SID and hashed password
Import-Module SqlServer

$SourceInstance = 'SOURCESERVER'
$OutFile        = 'C:\Migration\Logins.sql'

$srv = New-Object Microsoft.SqlServer.Management.Smo.Server $SourceInstance
$opts = New-Object Microsoft.SqlServer.Management.Smo.ScriptingOptions
$opts.LoginSid           = $true
$opts.IncludeIfNotExists = $true
$opts.ScriptDrops        = $false
$opts.AllowSystemObjects = $false

$lines = foreach ($login in $srv.Logins) {
    if ($login.Name -match '^(##|NT (SERVICE|AUTHORITY)\\)' -or
        $login.Name -eq 'sa') { continue }
    try { $login.Script($opts) } catch { "-- FAILED: $($login.Name) : $($_.Exception.Message)" }
}
$lines | Out-File -FilePath $OutFile -Encoding utf8
```

### 6.2 Server role memberships

```sql
SELECT
    r.name  AS role_name,
    r.type_desc AS role_type,
    m.name  AS member_name,
    m.type_desc AS member_type
FROM sys.server_role_members rm
JOIN sys.server_principals r ON rm.role_principal_id  = r.principal_id
JOIN sys.server_principals m ON rm.member_principal_id = m.principal_id
ORDER BY r.name, m.name;
```

### 6.3 Server-level permissions (GRANT/DENY)

```sql
SELECT
    pr.name           AS grantee,
    pr.type_desc      AS grantee_type,
    p.class_desc,
    p.permission_name,
    p.state_desc,
    CASE p.class
        WHEN 100 THEN 'SERVER'
        WHEN 101 THEN SUSER_NAME(p.major_id)
        WHEN 105 THEN OBJECT_NAME(p.major_id)  -- endpoint
        ELSE CAST(p.major_id AS VARCHAR(20))
    END AS secured_object
FROM sys.server_permissions p
JOIN sys.server_principals pr ON p.grantee_principal_id = pr.principal_id
ORDER BY pr.name, p.permission_name;
```

### 6.4 Database users, roles, permissions (per database)

```sql
-- Database users with login mapping and orphan detection
DECLARE @sql NVARCHAR(MAX) = N'';
SELECT @sql = @sql + N'
USE ' + QUOTENAME(name) + N';
SELECT DB_NAME() AS database_name,
       dp.name AS user_name,
       dp.type_desc,
       dp.default_schema_name,
       CONVERT(VARCHAR(MAX), dp.sid, 1) AS sid_hex,
       sp.name AS mapped_login,
       CASE WHEN dp.type IN (''S'',''U'',''G'') AND dp.authentication_type_desc = ''INSTANCE''
                 AND sp.sid IS NULL
            THEN ''ORPHANED'' ELSE ''OK'' END AS status
FROM sys.database_principals dp
LEFT JOIN sys.server_principals sp ON dp.sid = sp.sid
WHERE dp.principal_id > 4
  AND dp.type IN (''S'',''U'',''G'',''C'',''K'');'
FROM sys.databases WHERE state_desc = 'ONLINE' AND database_id > 4;
EXEC sp_executesql @sql;

-- Database role memberships
SET @sql = N'';
SELECT @sql = @sql + N'
USE ' + QUOTENAME(name) + N';
SELECT DB_NAME() AS database_name, r.name AS role_name, m.name AS member_name, m.type_desc
FROM sys.database_role_members rm
JOIN sys.database_principals r ON rm.role_principal_id  = r.principal_id
JOIN sys.database_principals m ON rm.member_principal_id = m.principal_id;'
FROM sys.databases WHERE state_desc = 'ONLINE' AND database_id > 4;
EXEC sp_executesql @sql;

-- Database-level permissions
SET @sql = N'';
SELECT @sql = @sql + N'
USE ' + QUOTENAME(name) + N';
SELECT DB_NAME() AS database_name,
       pr.name AS grantee,
       p.class_desc,
       p.permission_name,
       p.state_desc,
       OBJECT_SCHEMA_NAME(p.major_id) + ''.'' + OBJECT_NAME(p.major_id) AS object_name
FROM sys.database_permissions p
JOIN sys.database_principals pr ON p.grantee_principal_id = pr.principal_id
WHERE p.major_id > 0;'
FROM sys.databases WHERE state_desc = 'ONLINE' AND database_id > 4;
EXEC sp_executesql @sql;
```

### 6.5 Credentials

```sql
SELECT credential_id, name, credential_identity, create_date, modify_date, target_type, target_id
FROM sys.credentials;
```

Credential passwords cannot be scripted; you'll need them from your secrets store. Capture the names so nothing gets missed.

### 6.6 Linked servers and linked logins

```sql
SELECT
    s.server_id,
    s.name                  AS linked_server,
    s.product,
    s.provider,
    s.data_source,
    s.location,
    s.provider_string,
    s.catalog,
    s.is_remote_login_enabled,
    s.is_rpc_out_enabled,
    s.is_data_access_enabled,
    s.is_collation_compatible,
    s.uses_self_credential,
    s.lazy_schema_validation,
    s.is_system,
    s.connect_timeout,
    s.query_timeout,
    s.modify_date
FROM sys.servers s
WHERE s.server_id > 0;

SELECT
    s.name              AS linked_server,
    ll.uses_self_credential,
    sp.name             AS local_login,
    ll.remote_name,
    CASE WHEN ll.remote_password IS NULL THEN 'No password stored'
         ELSE 'Password stored (cannot script)' END AS password_status
FROM sys.linked_logins ll
JOIN sys.servers s            ON ll.server_id = s.server_id
LEFT JOIN sys.server_principals sp ON ll.local_principal_id = sp.principal_id
WHERE s.server_id > 0;
```

Linked server passwords are encrypted with the Service Master Key. Options: (a) re-create with passwords from your secrets store on the target; (b) back up the SMK from source and restore it on target *before* you create the linked servers (preserves password decryption). Method (a) is cleaner.

