## 13. POST-MIGRATION verification

Re-run all the capture scripts in §4–§11 against the target and diff against the source captures. Specific checks that warrant their own script:

### 13.1 Version and edition match expectations

```sql
SELECT @@VERSION, SERVERPROPERTY('Edition'), SERVERPROPERTY('ProductLevel'), SERVERPROPERTY('ProductUpdateLevel');
```

### 13.2 Database count and state

```sql
SELECT name, state_desc, recovery_model_desc, compatibility_level, collation_name
FROM sys.databases
ORDER BY name;
```

Diff against pre-migration list. Expected differences: `model`, `msdb`, `master` may differ in compat level if you've upgraded.

### 13.3 Login SID parity (catches the #1 migration bug)

Run this query on both source and target, then diff:

```sql
SELECT
    name,
    CONVERT(VARCHAR(MAX), sid, 1) AS sid_hex,
    type_desc, is_disabled, default_database_name
FROM sys.server_principals
WHERE type IN ('S','U','G')
  AND name NOT LIKE '##%'
ORDER BY name;
```

Any login on source whose SID doesn't match target = guaranteed orphaned users in any DB where that login owns objects or has database users.

### 13.4 Find and fix orphaned users

```sql
DECLARE @sql NVARCHAR(MAX) = N'';
SELECT @sql = @sql + N'
USE ' + QUOTENAME(name) + N';
SELECT DB_NAME() AS database_name, dp.name AS user_name,
       CONVERT(VARCHAR(MAX), dp.sid, 1) AS sid_hex, dp.type_desc
FROM sys.database_principals dp
LEFT JOIN sys.server_principals sp ON dp.sid = sp.sid
WHERE dp.type IN (''S'',''U'') AND sp.sid IS NULL
  AND dp.principal_id > 4;'
FROM sys.databases WHERE state_desc = 'ONLINE' AND database_id > 4;
EXEC sp_executesql @sql;
```

Fix by re-creating the login with matching SID (preferred — preserves permissions), or by remapping:

```sql
-- Per orphaned user, after the login is in place:
USE <database>;
ALTER USER <user_name> WITH LOGIN = <login_name>;
```

If you preserved SIDs in §6.1 and §13.3 is clean, you shouldn't have any of these.

### 13.5 Compatibility level decision

Default: keep the same compat level as source after upgrading the instance. To bump later, do so per database after performance testing.

```sql
-- Example: bump to 160 (SQL Server 2022)
ALTER DATABASE <name> SET COMPATIBILITY_LEVEL = 160;
```

Safety net if you bump and see regressions:

```sql
-- Force legacy cardinality estimator at db scope
ALTER DATABASE SCOPED CONFIGURATION SET LEGACY_CARDINALITY_ESTIMATION = ON;
-- Or use Query Store to force a previously good plan
```

### 13.6 Update statistics with FULLSCAN

```sql
DECLARE @sql NVARCHAR(MAX) = N'';
SELECT @sql = @sql + N'
USE ' + QUOTENAME(name) + N';
EXEC sp_updatestats;'  -- or per-table sp_updatestats / UPDATE STATISTICS ... WITH FULLSCAN
FROM sys.databases WHERE state_desc = 'ONLINE' AND database_id > 4;
EXEC sp_executesql @sql;
```

For thorough refresh (slower, IO-heavy):

```sql
-- Better: FULLSCAN, all stats
DECLARE @db SYSNAME, @sql NVARCHAR(MAX);
DECLARE c CURSOR LOCAL FAST_FORWARD FOR
    SELECT name FROM sys.databases WHERE state_desc = 'ONLINE' AND database_id > 4;
OPEN c; FETCH NEXT FROM c INTO @db;
WHILE @@FETCH_STATUS = 0
BEGIN
    SET @sql = N'USE ' + QUOTENAME(@db) + N';
    DECLARE @t SYSNAME, @s NVARCHAR(MAX);
    DECLARE tc CURSOR LOCAL FAST_FORWARD FOR
        SELECT QUOTENAME(SCHEMA_NAME(schema_id)) + ''.'' + QUOTENAME(name)
        FROM sys.tables;
    OPEN tc; FETCH NEXT FROM tc INTO @t;
    WHILE @@FETCH_STATUS = 0
    BEGIN
        SET @s = N''UPDATE STATISTICS '' + @t + N'' WITH FULLSCAN;'';
        EXEC sp_executesql @s;
        FETCH NEXT FROM tc INTO @t;
    END
    CLOSE tc; DEALLOCATE tc;';
    EXEC sp_executesql @sql;
    FETCH NEXT FROM c INTO @db;
END
CLOSE c; DEALLOCATE c;
```

### 13.7 DBCC UPDATEUSAGE

```sql
DECLARE @sql NVARCHAR(MAX) = N'';
SELECT @sql = @sql + N'DBCC UPDATEUSAGE(' + QUOTENAME(name) + N') WITH NO_INFOMSGS;
' FROM sys.databases WHERE state_desc = 'ONLINE' AND database_id > 4;
EXEC sp_executesql @sql;
```

### 13.8 DBCC CHECKDB WITH DATA_PURITY

```sql
DECLARE @sql NVARCHAR(MAX) = N'';
SELECT @sql = @sql + N'DBCC CHECKDB(' + QUOTENAME(name) + N') WITH DATA_PURITY, NO_INFOMSGS;
' FROM sys.databases WHERE state_desc = 'ONLINE' AND database_id > 4;
EXEC sp_executesql @sql;
```

For databases that originated on SQL 2005+ this is implicit. For older heritage databases, this catches stored values that are out of range for their declared types — a class of issue that older versions silently tolerated.

### 13.9 sp_refreshview for non-schemabound views

```sql
DECLARE @sql NVARCHAR(MAX) = N'';
SELECT @sql = @sql + N'
USE ' + QUOTENAME(name) + N';
DECLARE @v SYSNAME, @s NVARCHAR(MAX);
DECLARE vc CURSOR LOCAL FAST_FORWARD FOR
    SELECT QUOTENAME(SCHEMA_NAME(schema_id)) + ''.'' + QUOTENAME(name)
    FROM sys.views WHERE is_ms_shipped = 0
      AND OBJECTPROPERTY(object_id, ''IsSchemaBound'') = 0;
OPEN vc; FETCH NEXT FROM vc INTO @v;
WHILE @@FETCH_STATUS = 0
BEGIN
    BEGIN TRY EXEC sp_refreshview @v; END TRY
    BEGIN CATCH PRINT ''Failed to refresh: '' + @v + '' - '' + ERROR_MESSAGE(); END CATCH
    FETCH NEXT FROM vc INTO @v;
END
CLOSE vc; DEALLOCATE vc;'
FROM sys.databases WHERE state_desc = 'ONLINE' AND database_id > 4;
EXEC sp_executesql @sql;
```

### 13.10 Verify Agent jobs are enabled and have run

```sql
SELECT
    j.name,
    j.enabled,
    SUSER_SNAME(j.owner_sid) AS owner,
    (SELECT MAX(run_status_desc)
     FROM (SELECT TOP 1 CASE jh.run_status
                       WHEN 0 THEN 'Failed'
                       WHEN 1 THEN 'Succeeded'
                       WHEN 2 THEN 'Retry'
                       WHEN 3 THEN 'Cancelled'
                       WHEN 4 THEN 'In progress' END AS run_status_desc
           FROM msdb.dbo.sysjobhistory jh
           WHERE jh.job_id = j.job_id AND jh.step_id = 0
           ORDER BY jh.run_date DESC, jh.run_time DESC) x) AS last_outcome
FROM msdb.dbo.sysjobs j
ORDER BY j.name;
```

Owner check: any job owned by a domain account that doesn't exist on the new server context will silently misbehave. Re-own to `sa` or to a known service principal.

```sql
EXEC msdb.dbo.sp_update_job @job_name = N'...', @owner_login_name = N'sa';
```

### 13.11 Backups: take a fresh full and verify the chain

```sql
-- For each user database
BACKUP DATABASE <name>
TO DISK = N'<path>\<name>_postmigration_full.bak'
WITH COMPRESSION, CHECKSUM, INIT, STATS = 10;
```

Then verify your scheduled backups actually run on the new instance — don't assume.

### 13.12 Always On health post-migration / post-failover

```sql
-- Reuse §8.1 queries, plus:
SELECT * FROM sys.dm_hadr_cluster;
SELECT * FROM sys.dm_hadr_cluster_members;
SELECT * FROM sys.dm_hadr_cluster_networks;

-- Latency snapshot
SELECT
    DB_NAME(database_id) AS db,
    ar.replica_server_name,
    drs.log_send_queue_size,
    drs.log_send_rate,
    drs.redo_queue_size,
    drs.redo_rate,
    DATEDIFF(SECOND, drs.last_hardened_time, GETDATE()) AS seconds_since_last_hardened
FROM sys.dm_hadr_database_replica_states drs
JOIN sys.availability_replicas ar ON drs.replica_id = ar.replica_id;
```

